            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"An innumerable multitude, specifically referring to the vast, uncountable number of Abraham's descendants as promised by God.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Genesis 22:17\",\"Genesis 32:12\",\"Hosea 1:10\",\"Romans 9:27\",\"Hebrews 11:12\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Instability, foolishness, or a weak spiritual foundation for faith and life.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 7:26-27\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A divine boundary or limitation set by God to hold back chaotic forces or the sea.\",\"basis\":[\"Jeremiah 5:22\",\"Proverbs 8:29\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A heavy burden, overwhelming grief, or unbearable provocation.\",\"basis\":[\"Job 6:3\",\"Proverbs 27:3\"],\"prominence\":\"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"Sand functions as a prominent simile in Scripture. In the Old Testament, it overwhelmingly symbolizes an uncountable multitude, particularly in relation to the Abrahamic covenant. In the New Testament teachings of Jesus, it serves as a powerful metaphor for an unstable, worldly foundation compared to the solid rock of Christ's teachings.\"}",
